{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CQJ9Xr1BZe51vZQoSUBeR7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Code standard\n2\t\n3\tApplies to written or changed code, not instruction files. Covers how code reads, not what to build.\n4\t\n5\t1. **Never weaken a test to pass**: no deleting, skipping or loosening assertions. Fix the code or report the failure.\n6\t2. **Chesterton's Fence**: before deleting or materially changing non-obvious behavior, check call sites and history where likely to explain it.\n7\t3. **No comments**: add none except the allowed list; doubt means remove; change history goes in the commit.\n8\t4. **Allowed comments**: license header; behavior forced by an external library, platform or protocol, marked `why:` (`// why: ...`, `# why: ...`); doc comment of a public API; issue or RFC link.\n9\t5. **No commented-out code**: delete it. A \"do not remove\" or \"let op\" note becomes a test, type or lint rule.\n10\t6. **No suppressions**: no `eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` or alike. Only a pure style rule may be suppressed.\n11\t7. **Guard clauses**: handle the exit case first with an early return; keep the main path flat.\n12\t8. **Nesting**: at most 2 levels of control flow per function.\n13\t9. **No else after exit**: no `else` after `return`, `break`, `continue` or `throw`.\n14\t10. **No nested ternaries**: use a guard clause or a named function.\n15\t11. **No compact one-liners**: one step per line; no chained clever expressions.\n16\t12. **Explicit coercion**: convert types by name (`Number(x)`, `Boolean(x)`); no implicit coercion through `!!`, unary `+` or `==`.\n17\t13. **One responsibility**: one reason to change and one abstraction level per function.\n18\t14. **Length**: function over 20 lines is a review signal, not a failure. Split only along a real responsibility, into a name that adds meaning.\n19\t15. **Parameters**: at most 3; more → one object.\n20\t16. **Names**: intent-revealing domain names; no `data`, `info`, `result`, `helper`, `manager`, `process`, `temp`, no arbitrary abbreviations; single-letter name only as loop index or receiver in a scope under 10 lines.\n21\t17. **No forwarding abstractions**: no function, class or interface that only forwards to one caller without adding a boundary.\n22\t18. **Extract on the third copy**, or sooner when copies encode the same rule, not just the same shape.\n23\t19. **Failures**: handle at the right boundary; never swallow.\n24\t20. **Dead code**: remove only dead code, imports and variables your change orphaned; report other dead code.\n25\t21. **Architecture boundaries**: behavior in the owning layer; dependencies toward established contracts.\n26\t22. **No shortcuts**: no cycles, cross-layer shortcuts, duplicate sources of truth, mutable global state.\n27\t23. **Conventions**: match the surrounding file's naming, layout and idioms unless a rule here forbids them; follow conventions for errors, cancellation, concurrency, resource lifetimes.\n28\t24. **No inner guards**: no guard, fallback or try/catch for a state the code's own boundary already rules out; validate once at the trust boundary.\n29\t25. **Types rule out invalid states**: types and data structures make an invalid state unrepresentable, not checked for.\n30\t26. **No test-fitted code**: no hard-coded value or special case that only makes a test input pass; fix the cause.\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0c698e7-e566-4a08-9a99-c2a2cd2a8b3a","uuid":"f4b4a8bb-8d1c-467a-bf16-e8a0ae1b6154","timestamp":"2026-10-16T21:43:08.816Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/<user>/Documents/Code/personal/plugins/exo/skills/route-skills/references/code-standard.md","content":"# Code standard\n\nApplies to written or changed code, not instruction files. Covers how code reads, not what to build.\n\n1. **Never weaken a test to pass**: no deleting, skipping or loosening assertions. Fix the code or report the failure.\n2. **Chesterton's Fence**: before deleting or materially changing non-obvious behavior, check call sites and history where likely to explain it.\n3. **No comments**: add none except the allowed list; doubt means remove; change history goes in the commit.\n4. **Allowed comments**: license header; behavior forced by an external library, platform or protocol, marked `why:` (`// why: ...`, `# why: ...`); doc comment of a public API; issue or RFC link.\n5. **No commented-out code**: delete it. A \"do not remove\" or \"let op\" note becomes a test, type or lint rule.\n6. **No suppressions**: no `eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` or alike. Only a pure style rule may be suppressed.\n7. **Guard clauses**: handle the exit case first with an early return; keep the main path flat.\n8. **Nesting**: at most 2 levels of control flow per function.\n9. **No else after exit**: no `else` after `return`, `break`, `continue` or `throw`.\n10. **No nested ternaries**: use a guard clause or a named function.\n11. **No compact one-liners**: one step per line; no chained clever expressions.\n12. **Explicit coercion**: convert types by name (`Number(x)`, `Boolean(x)`); no implicit coercion through `!!`, unary `+` or `==`.\n13. **One responsibility**: one reason to change and one abstraction level per function.\n14. **Length**: function over 20 lines is a review signal, not a failure. Split only along a real responsibility, into a name that adds meaning.\n15. **Parameters**: at most 3; more → one object.\n16. **Names**: intent-revealing domain names; no `data`, `info`, `result`, `helper`, `manager`, `process`, `temp`, no arbitrary abbreviations; single-letter name only as loop index or receiver in a scope under 10 lines.\n17. **No forwarding abstractions**: no function, class or interface that only forwards to one caller without adding a boundary.\n18. **Extract on the third copy**, or sooner when copies encode the same rule, not just the same shape.\n19. **Failures**: handle at the right boundary; never swallow.\n20. **Dead code**: remove only dead code, imports and variables your change orphaned; report other dead code.\n21. **Architecture boundaries**: behavior in the owning layer; dependencies toward established contracts.\n22. **No shortcuts**: no cycles, cross-layer shortcuts, duplicate sources of truth, mutable global state.\n23. **Conventions**: match the surrounding file's naming, layout and idioms unless a rule here forbids them; follow conventions for errors, cancellation, concurrency, resource lifetimes.\n24. **No inner guards**: no guard, fallback or try/catch for a state the code's own boundary already rules out; validate once at the trust boundary.\n25. **Types rule out invalid states**: types and data structures make an invalid state unrepresentable, not checked for.\n26. **No test-fitted code**: no hard-coded value or special case that only makes a test input pass; fix the cause.\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CQJ9Xr1BZe51vZQoSUBeR7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
